Table: Products

+---------------+---------+
| Column Name   | Type    |
+---------------+---------+
| product_id    | int     |
| new_price     | int     |
| change_date   | date    |
+---------------+---------+
(product_id, change_date) is the primary key (combination of columns with unique values) of this table.
Each row of this table indicates that the price of some product was changed to a new price at some date.
Initially, all products have price 10.

Write a solution to find the prices of all products on the date 2019-08-16.
Return the result table in any order.
The result format is in the following example.

--- Way 1 --- 
=> I easy understand this way!

step1: select the new_price before or on 2019-08-16
step2: using coalesce to return the new_price if it exist, or 10 if there is no price change for the product
step3: group by product_id and take the last change_date: order by change_date max limit 1
step4: distinct product_id ensures that get only unique product_id

-- Write your PostgreSQL query statement below
select product_id, 
coalesce ((select new_price 
from Products np
where p.product_id = np.product_id and np.change_date <= '2019-08-16'
order by change_date desc
limit 1), 10) as price
from (select distinct product_id from Products order by product_id) p

